{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent high bias: the model level is systematically above realized prices by ~1–4 €/t on average, with the bias widening materially in the back half of the 30‑day horizon.",
    "Trend/turning-point lag: when the market transitions from rising to falling (or vice versa), the forecast continues the prior regime for too long; apply earlier inflection/mean-reversion than the model implies.",
    "Downside underreaction: in selloffs the model does not drop fast enough and keeps prices clustered near ~69–72 while outcomes slide into mid‑60s; increase negative drift when recent returns turn negative.",
    "Overshoot after spikes: following short-term highs (e.g., ~71–73), the model projects persistence/continued strength, but realized prices mean-revert sharply; cap post-spike forecasts and force reversion toward the recent 30‑day average.",
    "Horizon-dependent correction: small adjustment needed in days 1–10 (often slightly low during immediate continuation), but from ~day 15 onward apply a stronger downward correction and steeper decay toward a lower terminal level.",
    "Variance compression: forecasts are too smooth (range too narrow) relative to realized moves; allow larger downside tails, especially after volatility increases."
  ]
}
